<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William III of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1650">1650</a>–<a href="/years/1702">1702</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as William III of Orange, William of Orange</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William III, Prince of Orange, became stadtholder of Holland in 1672, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, and led the resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. In 1688 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and in 1689 he and his wife Mary II were proclaimed joint monarchs of England, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland. He led the coalition against France in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>, retaking Namur in 1695, and ruled until his death in 1702.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england.jpg" alt="William III of England" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Godfrey Kneller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_William_III_of_England,_(1650-1702).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
